{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"evileyed-nontoxic-urutu","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\n`infra/terraform/user_data.sh` has grown past AWS's hard **16,384-byte `user_data` limit**, so\n`terraform plan` FAILS TO GENERATE. No `terraform apply` is possible against this configuration —\nnot the alarm fixes, not anything.\n\n```\nError: expected length of user_data to be in the range (0 - 16384), got #!/usr/bin/env bash ...\n  with aws_instance.gerrit,\n  on main.tf line 88, in resource \"aws_instance\" \"gerrit\":\n  88:   user_data = templatefile(\"${path.module}/user_data.sh\", { ... })\n```\n\nMeasured on `origin/main` at `84ef284833be`:\n\n```\nuser_data.sh   16,666 bytes   (limit 16,384 — over by 282)\n               305 lines\n  comment bytes  12,169   (73%)\n  code bytes      4,497\n```\n\n## When it crossed\n\n```\n0033662b5534  2026-09-05  16,666  OVER   Assert every EBS mount actually took\ne6b4ff07da5d  2026-09-05  12,325         Refuse a degenerate EBS volume id\nbb65f47becfe  2026-09-04   9,519         Isolate review-gate scratch on its own EBS\n13e2ea6decc9  2026-08-28   6,461         dd30: unbreak terraform templatefile()\n30c92d4f43df  2026-06-29   4,713         S1 (5bfc): Terraform base\n```\n\nThe file has grown 3.5x in ten weeks and crossed the limit at `0033662b5534`.\n\n## Why it went unnoticed for a day — and a SEPARATE question\n\nThe daily `terraform-drift.yml` check has failed on 09-03, 09-04 and 09-05. Only the 09-05 failure\ncan be explained by this overflow (the file was 12,325 bytes before that day's commits). **The\n09-03 and 09-04 failures predate the crossing and have a DIFFERENT, still-undiagnosed cause** —\nmost plausibly genuine drift, i.e. real unapplied configuration. Do not assume one explanation\ncovers all three; whoever fixes this should confirm what the earlier two were, because a genuine\ndrift backlog is its own finding.\n\nThe reason nobody caught it: a red drift run reads as \"there is drift\". It is not distinguished\nfrom \"the plan could not be generated at all\", which is a different and much worse condition. That\nis the same conflation class as `erratic-sunny-genet` (Mirror Guard reports `healthy:false` when it\ncould not reach the host) and `deistic-conventual-blackmamba` (CI votes `Verified-1 FAILURE` on a\ncancelled run).\n\n## Suggested direction — and a SERIOUS hazard to check first\n\nThe obvious remedy is gzip: `user_data_base64 = base64gzip(templatefile(...))`. cloud-init accepts\ngzipped user-data, and at 73% comments this file compresses very well, so it buys years of headroom\nwithout deleting the documentation. Note the comments are NOT merely decorative here — the file\nitself warns that `templatefile()` interpolates the WHOLE file including comments, which is bug\n`dd30`'s subject, so they are load-bearing context for anyone editing it.\n\n**HAZARD, verify before applying anything:** changing `user_data` on `aws_instance` can FORCE\nINSTANCE REPLACEMENT. Replacing `aws_instance.gerrit` would destroy the production Gerrit host.\n`user_data_replace_on_change` defaults to `false` on modern AWS provider versions, which should\nmake this an in-place attribute update that only takes effect on next boot — but that MUST be\nconfirmed from the actual plan output (`0 to destroy`, and `aws_instance.gerrit` showing `update`\nrather than `replace`) before any apply. Do not take the default on trust.\n\nAlternatives if gzip is unsuitable: move the script to S3 and have a minimal bootstrap fetch it, or\nsplit the comment block into a repo-side doc with a pointer. Gzip is preferred because it preserves\nthe in-file documentation, which is the thing the size pressure would otherwise destroy.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 `terraform plan` GENERATES successfully against `origin/main`.\n- [ ] AC2 The plan shows `0 to destroy` and `aws_instance.gerrit` is NOT replaced. Proven from plan\n      output, not from the provider default.\n- [ ] AC3 The rendered user-data still works: cloud-init accepts the encoding and the script's\n      behaviour on boot is unchanged.\n- [ ] AC4 A guard prevents recurrence — the size is checked before it reaches AWS, so the next\n      contributor learns at commit or CI time rather than by a broken plan. State whether the guard\n      measures the RAW file or the RENDERED/encoded payload, and justify which is correct.\n- [ ] AC5 The distinction between \"plan failed to generate\" and \"drift detected\" is visible in the\n      drift workflow's output, so this class cannot hide as drift again.\n- [ ] AC6 The 09-03 and 09-04 drift failures are explained, or explicitly recorded as unexplained.\n\n## Constraints\n\nNever use `--force` or any gate bypass. `timeout` does not exist on this macOS host (exit 127) —\nbound with `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV'`. Do not touch the shared main checkout; do not\n`git stash` anywhere.","id":"a68c-9633-248c-4b06","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"user_data.sh exceeds AWS 16KiB limit: terraform plan cannot generate, ALL applies blocked"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788646074513100001,"uuid":"9db6efd7-d9de-4fe4-ab90-a3e1befff7f3"}